  "reason": "Evidence: RNA (pca_n2000, singleprofile) achieves 48.5% accuracy while image (resnet50, singleprofile) is very weak at 20.8%, establishing RNA as the stronger modality. However, we have only one RNA data point, so we cannot yet tell whether the profile setting (single vs. multi) is a meaningful design lever. Question: Does aggregating multiple profiles with a simple average pool improve RNA performance over the single-profile baseline? This isolates one variable (profile_setting + aggregation) while holding the RNA encoder fixed at pca_n2000. Why this is the best next experiment: It directly extends our best result with the minimal change, giving a clean read on whether multiprofile adds value. If avgpool helps, it validates the multiprofile branch and justifies testing transformer aggregation next; if it does not help, we can deprioritize multiprofile configurations and focus budget on encoder choice or multimodal fusion. This single experiment reduces uncertainty about a design axis that affects roughly half of the remaining configuration space."
}

